    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener><fw place="right" type="adressline">Parkway Community Church <lb/>209 Ampere
                            Parkway<lb/><rs ref="#tillich_place_id__980" type="place"
                            >Bloomfield</rs>, <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__867" type="place">New
                            Jersey</rs></fw>
                    <dateline><date when="1946-08-14">August 14, 1946</date></dateline>
                    <salute>Dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Dr.
                        Tillich</rs>:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>It is understood at seminary that <date when="1946-08-20">August 20</date>, is
                    your birthday. I am writing to add my congratulations and appreciation to that
                    of many students who have sat at your feet.</p>
                <p>There would be no exaggeration in the statement that you have stimulated the
                    thinking of the Union student body through the years in a unique way. That has
                    certainly been my own experience. Specifically, the concept of God as Ground of
                    Existence has enriched my American liberal personalistic view of God. Your
                    emphasis upon the importance of considering every area of life theologically has
                    lent great significance to my doctrine of general revelation and my ethical
                    understanding of stewardship. The correlations in your thinking, between the
                    findings of the natural sciences and the theological expressions of Christianity
                    has often stimulated me to seek new relationships between science and
                    religion.</p>
                <p>While there are many facets of your thought that I have yet to understand, the
                    practical outworking of your teaching has been a more catholic and a more open
                    approach to the issues of life, for me. What other students have said bears out
                    that testimony to your instruction. I pray that your most creative years stretch
                    on ahead of you.</p>
                <closer><salute>Sincerely, </salute><signed><rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3751"
                            type="person">Robert H. Bonthius</rs></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
